#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186120341 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2448ae49-d644-566d-b4ae-617f4c26be39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Capacitor Charge in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bb542746-c55a-5c6f-8a8c-4f5604d16c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316176}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bb542746-c55a-5c6f-8a8c-4f5604d16c42" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3}{8}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316176} :answer-field/id #uuid "c06a3a52-28bd-59ee-a7cc-c175a5c8973c" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "f22963ff-b55b-5a8b-88e3-83527086fce7" :question/math-academy-id "q-345014" :question/problem "A series RCL circuit has a source electromotive force (EMF) of $9\\,\\text{V},$ a resistance of $34\\,Ω,$ a capacitance of $\\frac{1}{24}\\,\\text{F},$ and an inductance of $L=12\\,\\text{H}.$ Find the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\n*Hint: The voltage drop ${V}_{C}$ across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C},$ where $C$ is the capacitance measured in Farads.*\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The diagram for our circuit is shown below.\n\n![](images/d8/d8303b679d4e00bac0f59f51941a2842473e050033f87f56f099b853431ac5aa.png)\n\nThe current $I,$ measured in amperes, is defined as the rate of flow of charge $q,$ measured in coulombs.\n\n$$\nI=\\frac{dq}{dt}\n$$\n\nKirchhoff's voltage law states that in a series circuit, the source voltage (EMF) equals the sum of the voltage drops. In an RCL circuit, there are three voltage drops:\n\n- The voltage drop across the resistor is proportional to the current, with a constant of proportionality $R=34\\,Ω.$ So,\n\n$$\n{V}_{R}=34I=34\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the capacitor is related to its charge by $q=C{V}_{C}.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n{V}_{C}=\\frac{q}{C}=\\frac{q}{(1/24)}=24q.\n$$\n\n- The voltage drop across the inductor is proportional to the rate of change of current, with a constant of proportionality $L=12\\,\\text{H}.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n{V}_{L}=12\\,\\frac{dI}{dt}=12\\,\\frac{d}{dt}(\\frac{dq}{dt})=12\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by Kirchhoff's voltage law, the differential equation governing the amount of charge $q$ on the capacitor is\n\n$$\n12\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+34\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+24q=9.\n$$\n\nThe *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0\\,\\text{and}\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nSubstituting these steady-state conditions into the differential equation gives\n\n$$\n12(0)+34(0)+24q=9,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n24q=9\\,⟹\\,q=\\frac{9}{24}=\\frac{3}{8}.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $\\frac{3}{8}$ coulombs."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186318976 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186119969 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2448ae49-d644-566d-b4ae-617f4c26be39" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Steady-State Capacitor Charge in an RCL Circuit: Constant EMF"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "69552e60-6c64-5b69-ae78-1dbd1d881d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "69552e60-6c64-5b69-ae78-1dbd1d881d62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "3" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317928} :answer-field/id #uuid "274bab22-c3a0-54d5-8b68-b9bd43f73177" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318503}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c7bd95ee-cca5-57b9-9825-7daba3c299ee" :question/math-academy-id "q-345122" :question/problem "The charge $q(t),$ in coulombs, in a series RCL circuit is governed by the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=24.\n$$\n\nFind the steady-state charge for this circuit.\n\nThe steady-state charge is\n{{field-1}}\ncoulombs." :question/worked-solution "The *steady-state charge* is the value that the charge $q(t)$ approaches as $t\\to \\infty .$ In a stable RCL circuit with a constant electromotive force, the transient response eventually decays to zero, leaving a constant charge.\n\nWhen the system reaches steady state, the charge $q$ is constant. Consequently, the first and second derivatives of the charge with respect to time are zero:\n\n$$\n\\frac{dq}{dt}=0,\\,\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the steady-state charge, we substitute these values into the given differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{d}^{2}q}{d{t}^{2}}+4\\,\\frac{dq}{dt}+8q=24\n$$\n\nSubstituting the steady-state conditions gives\n\n$$\n0+4(0)+8q=24,\n$$\n\nwhich simplifies to\n\n$$\n8q=24\\,⟹\\,q=3.\n$$\n\nPhysically, this represents the state where the capacitor is fully charged and the current $I=\\frac{dq}{dt}$ has ceased to flow.\n\nTherefore, the steady-state charge is $3$ coulombs."}]}
